Title: MoE hybrid mode (Strata-style): resident experts on the GPU, missed experts on the CPU at the same time
Add-on: replaces 0002 (ggml), 0003 (cache modes) and 0004 (draft KV). Off by default; stock behaviour is unchanged without the flags.
  --moe-hybrid-mib N          GPU memory for expert slots; every layer whose experts are in host memory (--cpu-moe / --n-cpu-moe) keeps
                              the same number of its most used experts on the GPU (CUDA / ROCm only)
  --moe-hybrid-max-tokens N   largest batch (1..64, default 64) that uses the hybrid form; larger batches (prompts) copy experts as before
  --moe-hybrid-swap-every N   generated tokens between two rebalances (default 16)
  --moe-hybrid-swap-max N     experts moved per rebalance while generating (default 32); after a prompt (64+ tokens) up to 2048
  --moe-hybrid-skip-weight F  skip CPU execution of experts whose router weight < F (default 0.05)
  --moe-hybrid-tail-skip N    skip CPU execution of experts whose rank >= N (default 5)
How a small batch runs (per MoE layer): the GPU looks up each selected expert in a slot table kept on the GPU and computes the resident ones;
the CPU computes the others from the host copy at the same time (the scheduler copies the ids and activations to the host right after the
router, with an event, instead of waiting for the whole GPU split); the two results are summed on the GPU. The Qwen3.5/3.6 MoE shared expert
is computed by the GPU while the CPU works. Experts move only between graph computes, by routing counts decayed by 0.7 at each rebalance; swaps are
chosen by additive score gain (cand >= victim + 1.5, floor 2.0) over all layers and copied asynchronously with lag-1 event completion.
Prompt batches copy the host experts to the GPU as before, but the ones with a slot are copied from GPU memory; their routing is counted in the copy callback.
ggml pieces: mul_mat_id skips ids < 0 and leaves zero rows (CPU, CUDA; fused paths disabled for them); ggml_backend_event_query (aborts on a
device fault); ggml_backend_sched_set_early_cpu_inputs (bounded, skipped with GGML_CUDA_GRAPH_OPT, keeps CUDA graph reuse via stable uids);
WDDM hit poke via event query on Windows forcing immediate concurrent GPU submission before CPU drain; Blackwell sm_120 MMVQ tuning.
Sequence state save (llama_io_write_host): neighbouring ranges of one device tensor are read as one span (gap <= 64 KiB, span <= 64 MiB,
host buffers untouched, direct into the output when contiguous). With several slots decoding into a unified KV cache a save was up to
18,100 single reads; measured on the 35B-A3B with 3 slots: stall to save a slot 484 -> 159 ms, idle-slot saves 406 -> 145 ms (mean),
byte-identical to the per-range read (53 saves compared). Idea from ggml-org/llama.cpp#30694, read amplification bounded.

